      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s most prominent visual landmark is the circa‑1910 water tower that proudly announces Faunsdale to passing motorists.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.faunsdalefoundation.org/about-faunsdale",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A railroad line reached Faunsdale in 1861, linking it to Selma, Dallas County, and Demopolis, and the station was named after Harrison’s home.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/faunsdale/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Faunsdale hosts the only Holdeman Mennonite Church outside Greensboro, home to a unique Mennonite community in the region.",
